==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*fimf}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*pénkʷe||five}}. Cognates include Old High German {{term|fimf|lang=goh}}, ''finf'' (German ''[[fünf]]''), Old Saxon {{term|fīf|lang=osx}}, Old Dutch ''fīf'', {{term|vīf|lang=odt}} (Dutch [[vijf]]), Old Norse ''[[fimm]]'' (Swedish ''[[fem]]''), Gothic {{term|𐍆𐌹𐌼𐍆|lang=got}}.  The Indo-European root is also the source of Greek {{term|πέντε|tr=pente|lang=el}}, Latin ''[[quinque]]'', Sanskrit {{term|पञ्चन्|lang=sa}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/fiːf/|lang=ang}}

===Numeral===
'''fīf'''

# {{context|cardinal|lang=ang}} [[five]]

